ncy changes traceable. Reliability and recovery
Define controller SLOs, reconciliation age, cluster onboarding, backup, upgrade, failure isolation, clean
bootstrap, drift response, rollback, and multi-cluster continuity. Architecture and Operating Decisions
Choose one primary GitOps controller and record the trade-off.
Use least privilege and bounded controller scope.
Verify rendered manifests and artifact identity before admission.
Run progressive delivery only with trustworthy metrics and abort behavior.
Test controller loss, stale source, bad configuration, unavailable cluster, and secret outage. Reference Configuration or Command Pattern
release_flow:
build: {artifact: sha256:<digest>, evidence: [tests, sbom, provenance, signature]}
promote: {environment_repo_change: reviewed}
reconcile: {controller: argocd-or-flux, policy: pass}
progress: {strategy: canary, analysis: [latency, errors, business_success]}
verify: {runtime_digest: match, user_path: pass}
recover: {abort_or_rollback: tested}
Production Readiness Checklist
☐ Ownership, trust boundaries, identities, inputs, outputs, and evidence are documented.
☐ Normal, negative, degraded, rollback, and recovery paths are tested.
☐ Changes are versioned, reviewed, reproducible, and independently verified.
☐ Secrets and privileged credentials are short-lived, least-privilege, and never embedded.
